---
description: Non-negotiable one-way isolation between framework and custom addons
alwaysApply: true
---

# Custom Addon Isolation

Custom addons are private consumers. Dependency direction is permanently:

`custom addon -> framework`

The reverse edge is forbidden. Tracked framework stays agnostic of any specific
custom addon someone might put in `addons/` (flat or under layout buckets such
as `enterprise/` / `community/`).

If you deleted every checkout under ignored `addons/`, a stranger reading
tracked framework (including tests) must still have **zero idea** those product
addons exist — by **name** and by **intent**. Layout bucket folder names alone
are not product identity; leaf package names and purpose still are.

## Absolute boundary

- No tracked file outside ignored `addons/` may identify a custom addon:
  source, tests, apps, workflows, Docker, docs, scripts, hooks, rules, skills,
  agents, fixtures, comments, migrations.
- Leakage is broader than imports: no `addons.<name>`,
  `@addons/<name>`, path/string literals, comments, fixtures, **or**
  identifiers/API purpose that only make sense for one product addon
  (variables, functions, classes, keys, routes, labels, visual effects). If
  deleting every custom checkout would leave a name or story that only served
  one product addon, it does not belong in tracked framework or base-addon code.
- Base addons (`framework/addons/`) must not reference custom addons. Declared
  base↔base edges are allowed when necessary; prefer generic discovery over
  hardcoded maps.
- CI/images validate framework-owned code only. Runtime may discover arbitrary
  addons and operator-supplied names; it must work with an empty `addons/`.

## How agents fail (and must stop)

The recurring bug is **pattern-matching the product task into substrate**:
copying product deep links, CDN paths, API routes, keys, labels, fill/paint
logic, or domain fixtures into `framework/**` “to prove the feature,” or
inventing a product-vocab denylist in tracked scripts (that denylist is itself
a leak).

**Conversation shape is never the control plane.** The user may freely mix
product and substrate topics in one thread. Do not ask them to split chats.
Do not add stop-hooks that fail merely because both trees were touched.
Isolation is **edit placement on every write**, every time.

Before every write under `framework/`, `scripts/`, `applications/`,
hand-authored `docs/`, or `.cursor/`:

1. Motivated by a product-addon scenario? → put it under `addons/<name>/`
   instead (or invent a fully generic contract with `example.*` / `alpha` /
   `beta` vocabulary only).
2. Would an empty-`addons/` stranger learn a product exists from this line?
   → do not write it.
3. About to list real product words in a scanner/denylist **or** in a
   rule/skill/agent/docs **example**? → do not. Policy examples use invented
   wording only (`addons/<name>/`, `example`, `alpha`/`beta`).
4. About to paste a real product URL, invite path, or attachment CDN path into
   a substrate test while implementing a generic GUI/API contract? → stop and
   invent `example` / `unbound` fixtures instead.
5. About to prove a reject/unsupported path by naming real third-party
   providers, brands, or content hosts in a substrate test (deny-list style
   negative fixtures)? → stop. That leaks app-level intent even when no
   custom-addon package name appears. Use invented hosts/values, or prove the
   allowlist-positive path only.

In any mixed request: product behavior/CSS/tests only under `addons/<name>/`;
substrate only generic hosts (forms, `overlays.renderer` delegation, discovery).

## Tests and scanners

- **Tests are isolation surfaces.** Framework / base-addon tests must pass the
  same empty-`addons/` stranger test as production code. Accidental leakage in
  fixtures, assert strings, comments, or parametrize tables is still leakage.
- Framework / base-addon tests run with no custom addons. Invented ids only
  prove **generic** substrate contracts. Reserved fixture vocabulary:
  `example`, `example_addon`, `example.*`, `alpha` / `beta`, `unbound`.
  Renaming fixtures to `example.*` does not clear purpose leakage if the
  motivating scenario is still a product feature.
- Prefer **allowlist / positive** fixtures for permitted behavior. For
  reject/unsupported cases, invent fake values (`https://video.unbound.example/…`,
  `example.record`, etc.). Do **not** maintain deny lists of real providers or
  product-domain hosts in tracked tests — that discloses what the substrate is
  built to police.
- `python3 scripts/addon/check_addon_isolation.py` (CI + stop hook) catches
  **structural identity** always (`addons/<non-framework>/`, imports) and, when
  ignored `addons/` checkouts are present locally, literal package-name path
  segments (including mid-path CDN URLs and `/api/<name>/…`). Never hard-code
  a product-vocabulary denylist there.
- Clean-checkout CI cannot see bare product strings (empty `addons/`). Before
  committing framework test or policy edits in a workspace that has custom
  addons checked out, agents **must** run
  `python3 scripts/addon/check_addon_isolation.py` and fix any hits. The stop hook
  re-runs that audit on touched files and fails the turn on violations.
- `.venv/bin/python scripts/addon/check_addon_dependencies.py` (dev) — release
  blocker; never allowlist.
- Purpose/intent isolation is agent judgment + the stranger test. Deslopify and
  modularity treat reverse identity **and** purpose leakage as Verified hygiene.
- Per-addon hygiene combines Isolation with a Substrate Check (hunt shared
  agnostic capability worth a `framework/addons/` extraction) before Deslop and
  Refactor — see `framework-addon-hygiene-checks`.
